## What was wrong

The `System.AsyncException: Maximum stack depth has been reached` came from my previous `execute()` method: it computed how many callouts it could still make from `Limits.getLimitCallouts() - Limits.getCallouts()` and, if it got zero (which is what happens in the test's synchronously executed job context), it treated *every* customer as "pending" and re-enqueued itself — which, in test context, re-enters `execute()` on the same stack, forever. It hit all three tests, including the single-account one.

Fix: **no runtime `Limits` arithmetic and no self-chaining at all.** The Accounts are split into chunks of 100 (the platform's callout limit) *up front*, in the trigger's transaction, and one job per chunk is enqueued. Each job simply sends its own customers — exactly one request per Account — and then ends. Since a marketplace batch is at most 200 Accounts, that is at most two jobs, and the loop is bounded so it can never recurse.

