/**
 * Sends renewal reminder emails to opportunity owners.
 *
 * IMPORTANT: Apex allows only 10 Messaging.sendEmail invocations per transaction,
 * so the emails MUST be bulkified into as few calls as possible (here: one call).
 * The per-message isolation that the original loop was trying to achieve comes from
 * passing allOrNothing = false, which makes sendEmail return one SendEmailResult per
 * message and never abort the whole batch because a single message failed.
 */
public with sharing class RenewalReminderService {

    /**
     * Emails the owner of each of the supplied opportunities a renewal reminder.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds opportunities whose renewals are due (the scheduled job
     *                       passes up to 200 ids per invocation)
     * @return the number of reminder emails that were sent successfully
     */
    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        List<Opportunity> opportunities = [
            SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds
        ];

        List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> messages = new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>();
        List<Id> messageOpportunityIds = new List<Id>();

        for (Opportunity opp : opportunities) {
            // setTargetObjectId requires a User, Contact or Lead id. Queue-owned
            // opportunities have no user owner, so they are skipped and logged.
            if (opp.OwnerId == null) {
                System.debug(LoggingLevel.WARN,
                    'Skipping renewal reminder for opportunity ' + opp.Id + ': no owner Id.');
                continue;
            }

            String closeDateText = (opp.CloseDate == null)
                ? 'not set'
                : opp.CloseDate.format();

            Messaging.SingleEmailMessage mail = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
            mail.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);
            mail.setSaveAsActivity(false); // emails to internal users don't hit the external allocation
            mail.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opp.Name);
            mail.setPlainTextBody(
                'The renewal for opportunity "' + opp.Name + '" is due.\n' +
                'Close date: ' + closeDateText + '.\n\n' +
                'Please review this opportunity and contact the customer.'
            );

            messages.add(mail);
            messageOpportunityIds.add(opp.Id);
        }

        if (messages.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        // ONE bulk call (1 of the 10 allowed invocations per transaction).
        // allOrNothing = false => each message gets its own result and a failure
        // on one message does not stop the others.
        Messaging.SendEmailResult[] results = Messaging.sendEmail(messages, false);

        Integer sentCount = 0;
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (results[i].isSuccess()) {
                sentCount++;
            } else {
                Id oppId = (i < messageOpportunityIds.size()) ? messageOpportunityIds[i] : null;
                for (Messaging.SendEmailError error : results[i].getErrors()) {
                    System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                        'Renewal reminder for opportunity ' + oppId + ' failed: ' + error.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }

        return sentCount;
    }
}
```

## Why the original approach is a bad idea

**1. One `Messaging.sendEmail` call per opportunity blows the invocation limit.**
Apex permits a maximum of **10 `Messaging.sendEmail` invocations per transaction**. Looping over 200 opportunities therefore fails with `System.LimitException: Too many Email Invocations: 11` partway through the run — the worst possible outcome, because the job dies mid-way, some owners get reminded, some don’t, and there is no clean retry point. (This limit is enforced in unit tests too, so a 200-record test will surface it.)

**2. Failure isolation does not require one call per message — it requires `allOrNothing = false`.**
`Messaging.sendEmail(emails, false)` already returns one `SendEmailResult` per message and never aborts the batch because one message failed. So the stated reason for the per-opportunity call is not actually a reason: you get identical isolation, plus per-message error reporting, from a single bulk call. This is the actual fix — bulkify, don’t loop.

**3. `Messaging.sendEmail` does not throw on individual failures.**
It returns `SendEmailResult[]` with `isSuccess()` / `getErrors()`. Wrapping calls in `try/catch` only catches hard platform errors (like the governor limit itself) and misses soft failures such as an invalid target Id. Inspect the results instead, and log the failed opportunity Ids so a partial run is visible and can be retried.

**4. `setTargetObjectId` needs a User, Contact or Lead Id — opportunity owners can be Queues.**
Queue-owned opportunities will fail at send time. Decide the business rule explicitly (skip and report, or notify a fallback user) rather than logging the same errors every Monday. Likewise guard `CloseDate` (null-safe) and inactive owners.

**5. `setSaveAsActivity(false)` is correct here.** Emails addressed to internal users by Id don’t consume the org’s *external* daily email allowance, and the org’s internal daily cap still applies, so a 200-email Monday run must be monitored — but this is not a bug.

**6. No audit trail / idempotency.**
With `setSaveAsActivity(false)` nothing is recorded on the opportunity. If the job is retried, re-run manually, or the scheduler hiccups, owners get duplicate reminders and you can’t tell who was already notified. Consider writing a lightweight log record (custom object or a completed Task) per send, and query it to suppress duplicates.

**7. `with sharing` is a deliberate choice.** The query honours the running user’s access. A scheduled job usually runs as an automated user, so make sure that user can see all due renewals — otherwise use `without sharing` knowingly, or run the job as a user with the right access. Don’t leave it to chance.

**8. If the volume ever grows beyond what one transaction can send, split the work across transactions** (a `Batchable` or `Queueable` handling a safe number of reminders per `execute`) rather than trying to force everything into one call.

